    "created_at": "2018-05-30T13:10:10Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-05-30T13:10:10Z",
    "body": "That's correct.  \"required\" is explicitly in _AttributeNameKeyword_ and _ArgumentNameKeyword_, precisely because it's unambiguous there and we don't want people to have to mess with escaping it.\r\n\r\nAnd yes, that is what the IDL parser in Firefox implements:\r\n\r\n            AttributeName : IDENTIFIER\r\n                          | REQUIRED\r\n\r\nLooking at the ticket that started all this, it starts off with https://github.com/w3c/webidl2.js/pull/157 \r\n\r\nAnd looking at https://github.com/w3c/webidl2.js/blob/835e835461a5df880bf62fd92094a6c7eee142d3/lib/webidl2.js as of today, it has an \"argumentNameKeywords\" array which it uses for parsing the `argument_name` production down at <https://github.com/w3c/webidl2.js/blob/835e835461a5df880bf62fd92094a6c7eee142d3/lib/webidl2.js#L431>.  But for parsing attributes what it does in the `attribute_rest` production is to consume the literal \"attribute\", then consume a `type_with_extended_attributes`, then consume an identifier.  That doesn't match the IDL grammar, which has:\r\n\r\n     attribute TypeWithExtendedAttributes AttributeName ;",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
